== THE SECOND STAGE

So I saw in my dream that they walked on their way, and had very comfortable weather.

Then Christiana began to sing, saying,

#quote[Blessed be the day that I began \
A pilgrim for to be; \
And blessed also be the man \
That thereto moved me.]

#quote[’Tis true, ’twas long ere I began \
To seek to live for ever; \
But now I run fast as I can: \
’Tis better late than never.]

#quote[Our tears to joy, our fears to faith, \
Are turned, as we see; \
Thus our beginning (as one saith) \
Shows what our end will be.]

Now there was, on the other side of the wall that fenced in the way up which Christiana and her companions were to go, a garden, and that garden belonged to him who owned that barking dog of whom mention was made before. And some of the fruit trees that grew in that garden shot their branches over the wall; and the fruit being mellow, those who found it gathered it up, and ate it to their hurt. So Christiana’s boys, as boys are apt to do, being pleased with the trees, and with the fruit that hung on them, plucked it, and began to eat. Their mother also scolded them for doing so; but still the boys went on.

Well, she said, my sons, you transgress, for that fruit is not ours; but she did not know that it belonged to the enemy: I’ll warrant you, if she had, she would have been ready to die for fear. But that passed, and they went on their way. Now, by the time they had gone about two bow-shots from the place that led them into the way, they saw two very ill-favored ones coming down quickly to meet them. With that, Christiana and Mercy her friend covered themselves with their veils, and so kept on their journey: the children also went on ahead; so that at last they met together. Then those who came down to meet them came right up to the women, as if they would embrace them; but Christiana said, Stand back, or go peaceably as you should. Yet these two, like men who are deaf, paid no attention to Christiana’s words, but began to lay hands on them: at that Christiana, growing very angry, kicked at them with her feet. Mercy also, as well as she could, did what she could to fend them off. Christiana again said to them, Stand back, and be gone, for we have no money to lose, being pilgrims, as you see, and such too as live on the charity of our friends.

#speech[#smallcaps[Ill-Favored Ones]: Then one of the two men said, We are not attacking you for money, but have come out to tell you that if you will only grant one small request which we shall ask, we will make women of you forever.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Now Christiana, imagining what they meant, answered again, We will neither hear, nor regard, nor yield to what you shall ask. We are in haste, and cannot stay; our business is a business of life and death. So again she and her companion made a fresh attempt to get past them; but they hindered them in their way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Ill-Favored Ones]: And they said, We intend no harm to your lives; it is another thing we would have.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Yes, said Christiana, you would have us body and soul, for I know it is for that you have come; but we will rather die on the spot than allow ourselves to be brought into such snares as shall endanger our well-being hereafter. And with that they both shrieked out, and cried, Murder! murder! and so put themselves under those laws that are provided for the protection of women. Deut 22:25–27. But the men still kept closing in on them, intending to prevail against them. They therefore cried out again.]

Now they being, as I said, not far from the gate by which they had come in, their voices were heard from where they were, back there: so some of the house came out, and knowing that it was Christiana’s voice, they hurried to her relief. But by the time they had come within sight of them, the women were in a very great scuffle; the children also stood crying by. Then he who came for their relief called out to the ruffians, saying, What is this thing you are doing? Would you make my Lord’s people transgress? He also tried to take them, but they made their escape over the wall into the garden of the man to whom the great dog belonged; so the dog became their protector. This Reliever then came up to the women, and asked them how they were. So they answered, We thank your Prince, pretty well, only we have been somewhat frightened: we thank you also for coming in to help us, otherwise we would have been overcome.

#speech[#smallcaps[Reliever]: So, after a few more words, this Reliever said as follows: I marveled much, when you#super[+] were received at the gate above, seeing you knew that you were only weak women, that you did not petition the Lord for a conductor; then you might have avoided these troubles and dangers; for he would have granted you one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Alas! said Christiana, we were so taken with our present blessing that dangers to come were forgotten by us. Besides, who could have thought that so near the King’s palace such wicked ones could have lurked? Indeed, it would have been well for us had we asked our Lord for one; but since our Lord knew it would be for our profit, I wonder that he did not send one along with us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Reliever]: It is not always necessary to grant things not asked for, lest by so doing they come to be little valued; but when the lack of a thing is felt, it then gains, in the eyes of him who feels it, the value that is properly its due, and so will afterward be used accordingly. Had my Lord granted you a conductor, you would not have so bewailed that oversight of yours, in not asking for one, as you now have occasion to do. So all things work for good, and tend to make you more wary.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Shall we go back again to my Lord, and confess our folly, and ask for one?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Reliever]: Your confession of your folly I will present to him. You need not go back again, for in all places where you shall come, you will find no lack at all; for in every one of my Lord’s lodgings, which he has prepared for the reception of his pilgrims, there is enough to equip them against all attacks whatever. But, as I said, He will be inquired of by them, to do it for them. Ezek 36:37. And it is a poor thing that is not worth asking for. When he had said this, he went back to his place, and the pilgrims went on their way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy said, What a sudden disappointment this is! I had reckoned that we were past all danger, and that we would never see sorrow again.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Your innocence, my sister, said Christiana to Mercy, may excuse you much; but as for me, my fault is so much the greater, because I saw this danger before I came out of the doors, and yet did not provide for it when provision might have been had. I am much to be blamed.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy said, How did you know this before you came from home? Please explain this riddle to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Why, I will tell you. Before I set foot out of doors, one night as I lay in my bed I had a dream about this; for I thought I saw two men, as like these as any in the world could ever look, standing at the foot of my bed, plotting how they might prevent my salvation. I will tell you their very words. They said (it was when I was in my troubles), What shall we do with this woman? for she cries out, waking and sleeping, for forgiveness: if she is allowed to go on as she begins, we shall lose her as we have lost her husband. This, you know, might have made me take heed, and provide when provision might have been had.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Well, said Mercy, as by this neglect we have been given an occasion to see our own imperfections, so our Lord has taken occasion by it to make known the riches of his grace; for he, as we see, has followed us with unasked kindness, and has delivered us, of his sheer good pleasure, from the hands of those who were stronger than we.]

So now, when they had talked away a little more time, they drew near to a house which stood in the way, which house was built for the relief of pilgrims, as you will find more fully related in the first part of these records of the Pilgrim’s Progress. So they drew on toward the house (the house of the Interpreter); and when they came to the door, they heard a great deal of talk in the house. Then they listened, and heard, as they thought, Christiana mentioned by name; for you must know that a report of her and her children’s going on pilgrimage had gone along even before her. And this was the more pleasing to them, because they had heard that she was Christian’s wife, that woman who some time ago was so unwilling to hear of going on pilgrimage. So they stood still, and heard the good people within praising her, though they little thought that she stood at the door. At last Christiana knocked, as she had done at the gate before. Now, when she had knocked, a young damsel came to the door, and opened the door, and looked, and behold, two women were there.

#speech[#smallcaps[The Damsel]: Then the damsel said to them, With whom would you speak in this place?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Christiana answered, We understand that this is a privileged place for those who have become pilgrims, and we now at this door are such: so we pray that we may be partakers of that for which we have now come; for the day, as you see, is very far spent, and we are unwilling to go any further tonight.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Damsel]: Please, what may I call your name, so that I may tell it to my Lord within?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: My name is Christiana; I was the wife of that pilgrim who some years ago traveled this way, and these are his four children. This maiden also is my companion, and is going on pilgrimage too.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Innocent]: Then Innocent ran in (for that was her name), and said to those within, Can you guess who is at the door? There is Christiana and her children, and her companion, all waiting for a welcome here. Then they leaped for joy, and went and told their Master. So he came to the door, and looking at her, he said, Are you that Christiana whom Christian the good man left behind him when he took himself to a pilgrim’s life?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: I am that woman who was so hard-hearted as to slight my husband’s troubles, and who left him to go on in his journey alone, and these are his four children; but now I also have come, for I am convinced that no way is right but this.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Then that is fulfilled which is written of the man who said to his son, “Go and work today in my vineyard; and he said to his father, ‘I will not’; but afterward he repented and went.” Matt 21:29.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana said, So be it: Amen. God make it a true saying of me, and grant that I may be found by him at the last in peace, without spot, and blameless.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: But why do you stand like this at the door? Come in, daughter of Abraham; we were talking of you just now, for news had come to us ahead of you of how you had become a pilgrim. Come, children, come in; come, maiden, come in. So he took them all into the house.]

So when they were inside, they were invited to sit down and rest; and when they had done so, those who attended on the pilgrims in the house came into the room to see them. And one smiled, and another smiled, and they all smiled for joy that Christiana had become a pilgrim. They also looked at the boys; they stroked their faces with the hand, in token of their kind reception of them: they also behaved lovingly toward Mercy, and made them all welcome into their Master’s house.

After a while, because supper was not ready, the Interpreter took them into his Significant Rooms, and showed them what Christian, Christiana’s husband, had seen some time before. Here, therefore, they saw the man in the cage, the man and his dream, the man who cut his way through his enemies, and the picture of the biggest of them all, together with the rest of those things that were then so profitable to Christian.

This done, and after those things had been somewhat digested by Christiana and her company, the Interpreter takes them aside again, and takes them first into a room where there was a man who could look no way but downward, with a muck-rake in his hand. There stood also one over his head with a celestial crown in his hand, and he offered him that crown for his muck-rake; but the man neither looked up nor paid any attention, but raked to himself the straws, the small sticks, and the dust of the floor.

Then Christiana said, I am persuaded that I know something of the meaning of this; for this is a figure of a man of this world: is it not, good sir?

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: You have said right, he said; and his muck-rake shows his carnal mind. And whereas you see him rather give heed to raking up straws and sticks, and the dust of the floor, than to do what He says who calls to him from above with the celestial crown in his hand; it is to show that heaven is only a fable to some, and that things here are counted the only substantial things. Now, whereas it was also shown you that the man could look no way but downward, it is to let you know that earthly things, when they lie with power on men’s minds, quite carry their hearts away from God.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana said, O deliver me from this muck-rake. Prov 30:8.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: That prayer, said the Interpreter, has lain by till it is almost rusty: “Do not give me riches,” is scarcely the prayer of one in ten thousand. Straws, and sticks, and dust, with most people, are the great things now looked after.]

With that Christiana and Mercy wept, and said, It is, alas! too true.

When the Interpreter had shown them this, he took them into the very best room in the house; a very fine room it was. So he told them to look all around, and see if they could find anything profitable there. Then they looked round and round; for there was nothing to be seen but a very great spider on the wall, and that they overlooked.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy said, Sir, I see nothing; but Christiana held her peace.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: But, said the Interpreter, look again. She therefore looked again, and said, There is nothing here but an ugly spider, who hangs by her hands on the wall. Then he said, Is there only one spider in all this spacious room? Then the water stood in Christiana’s eyes, for she was a woman quick to understand; and she said, Yes, Lord, there are more here than one; yes, and spiders whose venom is far more destructive than that which is in her. The Interpreter then looked pleasantly on her, and said, You have said the truth. This made Mercy blush, and the boys cover their faces; for they all now began to understand the riddle.]

Then the Interpreter said again, “The spider takes hold with her hands,” as you see, “and is in kings’ palaces.” Prov 30:28. And why is this recorded, but to show you that, however full of the venom of sin you may be, yet you may, by the hand of Faith, lay hold of and dwell in the best room that belongs to the King’s house above?

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: I thought, said Christiana, of something of this; but I could not imagine it at all. I thought that we were like spiders, and that we looked like ugly creatures, in whatever fine room we were: but that by this spider, that venomous and ill-favored creature, we were to learn how to exercise faith, that did not come into my thoughts; and yet she had taken hold with her hands, and, as I see, dwells in the best room in the house. God has made nothing in vain.]

Then they all seemed to be glad; but the water stood in their eyes; yet they looked at one another, and also bowed before the Interpreter.

He took them into another room, where there were a hen and chickens, and told them to watch a while. So one of the chickens went to the trough to drink, and every time she drank she lifted up her head and her eyes toward heaven. See, he said, what this little chick does, and learn from her to acknowledge where your mercies come from, by receiving them with looking up. Yet again, he said, observe and look: so they gave heed, and saw that the hen walked in a fourfold method toward her chickens: 1. She had a common call, and that she has all day long. 2. She had a special call, and that she had only at times. 3. She had a brooding note. Matt 23:37. And, 4. She had an outcry.

Now, he said, compare this hen to your King, and these chickens to his obedient ones; for, answering to her, he himself has his methods which he walks in toward his people. By his common call, he gives nothing; by his special call, he always has something to give; he has also a brooding voice, for those who are under his wing; and he has an outcry, to give the alarm when he sees the enemy come. I choose, my darlings, to lead you into the room where such things are, because you are women, and they are easy for you.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: And, sir, said Christiana, please let us see some more. So he took them into the slaughterhouse, where a butcher was killing a sheep; and behold, the sheep was quiet, and took her death patiently. Then the Interpreter said, You must learn from this sheep to suffer and to put up with wrongs without murmurings and complaints. See how quietly she takes her death, and, without objecting, she allows her skin to be pulled over her ears. Your King calls you his sheep.]

After this he led them into his garden, where there was a great variety of flowers; and he said, Do you see all these? So Christiana said, Yes. Then he said again, See, the flowers are different in height, in quality, and color, and smell, and virtue; and some are better than others; also, where the gardener has set them, there they stand, and do not quarrel with one another.

Again, he took them into his field, which he had sown with wheat and other grain: but when they looked, the tops of all were cut off, and only the straw remained. He said again, This ground was manured, and plowed, and sown, but what shall we do with the crop? Then Christiana said, Burn some, and make muck of the rest. Then the Interpreter said again, Fruit, you see, is the thing you look for; and for lack of that you condemn it to the fire, and to be trodden underfoot by men: beware that in this you do not condemn yourselves.

Then, as they were coming in from outside, they saw a little robin with a great spider in his mouth. So the Interpreter said, Look here. So they looked, and Mercy wondered, but Christiana said, What a discredit this is to such a pretty little bird as the robin redbreast; he being also a bird that, above many, loves to keep up a kind of sociableness with men! I had thought they lived on crumbs of bread, or on other such harmless matter: I like him worse than I did.

The Interpreter then replied, This robin is an emblem, very apt to set forth some professing Christians by; for to look at they are, like this robin, pretty in song, color, and bearing. They seem also to have a very great love for sincere professing Christians; and, above all others, to desire to associate with them, and to be in their company, as if they could live on the good man’s crumbs. They also pretend that this is why they frequent the house of the godly and the ordinances of the Lord: but when they are by themselves, like the robin, they can catch and gobble up spiders; they can change their diet, drink iniquity, and swallow down sin like water.

So, when they had come into the house again, because supper was not ready yet, Christiana again asked that the Interpreter would either show or tell some other things that are profitable.

Then the Interpreter began, and said, The fatter the sow is, the more she desires the mire; the fatter the ox is, the more playfully he goes to the slaughter; and the more healthy the lustful man is, the more prone he is to evil. There is a desire in women to go neat and fine; and it is a becoming thing to be adorned with that which in God’s sight is of great price. It is easier to watch a night or two than to sit up a whole year together: so it is easier for one to begin to profess well than to hold out as he should to the end. Every shipmaster, when in a storm, will willingly throw overboard what is of the smallest value in the vessel; but who will throw the best out first? No one but he who does not fear God. One leak will sink a ship, and one sin will destroy a sinner. He who forgets his friend is ungrateful to him; but he who forgets his Savior is unmerciful to himself. He who lives in sin, and looks for happiness hereafter, is like him who sows weeds, and thinks to fill his barn with wheat or barley. If a man would live well, let him fetch his last day to him, and make it always his company-keeper. Whispering, and change of thoughts, prove that sin is in the world. If the world, which God sets little by, is counted a thing of such worth by men, what is heaven, which God commends? If the life that is attended with so many troubles is so unwillingly let go by us, what is the life above? Everybody will praise the goodness of men; but who is there that is, as he should be, moved by the goodness of God? We seldom sit down to a meal but we eat, and leave some. So there is in Jesus Christ more merit and righteousness than the whole world has need of.

When the Interpreter had finished, he takes them out into his garden again, and took them to a tree whose inside was all rotten and gone, and yet it grew and had leaves. Then Mercy said, What does this mean? This tree, he said, whose outside is fair, and whose inside is rotten, is that to which many may be compared who are in the garden of God; who with their mouths speak high on behalf of God, but in fact will do nothing for him; whose leaves are fair, but their heart good for nothing but to be tinder for the devil’s tinderbox.

Now supper was ready, the table spread, and everything set on the board: so they sat down and ate, when one had given thanks. And the Interpreter usually entertained those who lodged with him with music at meals; so the minstrels played. There was also one who sang, and a very fine voice he had. His song was this:

#quote[“The Lord is only my support, \
And he that doth me feed; \
How can I then want any thing \
Whereof I stand in need?”]

When the song and music were ended, the Interpreter asked Christiana what it was that had first moved her to take up a pilgrim’s life in this way. Christiana answered, First, the loss of my husband came into my mind, at which I was heartily grieved; but all that was only natural affection. Then after that the troubles and pilgrimage of my husband came into my mind, and also how churlishly I had behaved toward him in that. So guilt took hold of my mind, and would have drawn me into the pond, but that, at the right moment, I had a dream of the well-being of my husband, and a letter was sent me by the King of that country where my husband dwells, to come to him. The dream and the letter together so worked on my mind that they forced me to this way.

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: But did you meet with no opposition before you set out of doors?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Yes, a neighbor of mine, one Mrs. Timorous: she was related to him who would have persuaded my husband to go back, for fear of the lions. She also called me a fool for, as she called it, my intended desperate adventure; she also urged whatever she could to dishearten me from it, the hardships and troubles that my husband met with in the way; but all this I got over pretty well. But a dream that I had of two ill-looking ones, who I thought were plotting how to make me fail in my journey, has troubled me much: yes, it still runs in my mind, and makes me afraid of everyone that I meet, lest they should meet me to do me harm, and to turn me out of my way. Yes, I may tell my Lord, though I would not have everybody know of it, that between here and the gate by which we got into the way, we were both so sorely assaulted that we were made to cry out murder; and the two who made this assault on us were like the two that I saw in my dream.]

Then the Interpreter said, Your beginning is good; your latter end shall greatly increase. So he turned to Mercy, and said to her, And what moved you to come here, sweet heart?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy blushed and trembled, and for a while stayed silent.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Then he said, Do not be afraid; only believe, and speak your mind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: So she began, and said, Truly, sir, my lack of experience is what makes me wish to keep silent, and that also is what fills me with fears of coming short at last. I cannot tell of visions and dreams, as my friend Christiana can; nor do I know what it is to mourn for refusing the counsel of those who were good relations.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: What was it, then, dear heart, that has prevailed with you to do as you have done?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Why, when our friend here was packing up to leave our town, I and another happened to go to see her. So we knocked at the door and went in. When we were inside, and saw what she was doing, we asked her what she meant by it. She said she was sent for to go to her husband; and then she up and told us how she had seen him in a dream, dwelling in an exquisite place, among immortals, wearing a crown, playing on a harp, eating and drinking at his Prince’s table, and singing praises to him for bringing him there, etc. Now, I thought, while she was telling us these things, my heart burned within me. And I said in my heart, If this is true, I will leave my father and my mother, and the land of my birth, and will, if I may, go along with Christiana. So I asked her further about the truth of these things, and whether she would let me go with her; for I saw now that there was no living any longer in our town, except in danger of ruin. But still I came away with a heavy heart; not because I was unwilling to come away, but because so many of my relations were left behind. And I have come with all the desire of my heart, and will go, if I may, with Christiana to her husband and his King.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Your setting out is good, for you have given credit to the truth; you are a Ruth, who, for the love she bore to Naomi and to the Lord her God, left father and mother, and the land of her birth, to come out and go with a people she had not known before. “May the #smallcaps[Lord] repay your work, and may you receive a rich reward from the #smallcaps[Lord], the God of Israel, under whose wings you have taken refuge.” Ruth 2:11, 12.]

Now supper was ended, and preparation was made for bed; the women were each laid alone, and the boys by themselves. Now when Mercy was in bed, she could not sleep for joy, because now her fears of missing at the last were removed further from her than they ever had been before. So she lay blessing and praising God, who had such favor for her.

In the morning they rose with the sun, and prepared themselves for their departure; but the Interpreter wanted them to stay a while; for, he said, you must go from here in proper order. Then he said to the damsel who first opened to them, Take them into the garden to the bath, and there wash them and make them clean from the dirt which they have gathered by traveling. Then Innocent the damsel took them and led them into the garden, and brought them to the bath; so she told them that there they must wash and be clean, for so her Master would have the women do who called at his house as they were going on pilgrimage. Then they went in and washed, yes, they and the boys, and all; and they came out of that bath not only sweet and clean, but also much enlivened and strengthened in their joints. So when they came in, they looked a great deal fairer than when they went out to the washing.

When they had returned from the bath in the garden, the Interpreter took them and looked at them, and said to them, “As fair as the moon.” Then he called for the seal with which those who were washed in his bath used to be sealed. So the seal was brought, and he set his mark on them, that they might be known in the places where they were still to go. Now the seal was the contents and sum of the passover which the children of Israel ate, Exod 13:8–10, when they came out of the land of Egypt; and the mark was set between their eyes. This seal greatly added to their beauty, for it was an ornament to their faces. It also added to their gravity, and made their countenance more like those of angels.

Then the Interpreter said again to the damsel who waited on these women, Go into the vestry, and fetch out garments for these people. So she went and fetched out white garments, and laid them down before him; so he commanded them to put them on: it was fine linen, white and clean. When the women were adorned like this, they seemed to be a terror to one another; for they could not see in themselves the glory each one had, which they could see in each other. Now therefore they began to esteem each other better than themselves. For, You are fairer than I am, said one; and, You are more comely than I am, said another. The children also stood amazed to see into what fashion they had been brought.
